    ],
    "word_count": 50,
    "html": "<p>Hans-Günther Schmid was a German entrepreneur whose alloy-wheel businesses financed his involvement in Formula One. He helped build ATS before exchanging his stake for Rial and its factory at Fußgönheim. His son Ralf and nephew Michael later took over management, continuing the family's involvement in wheel manufacturing after his withdrawal.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Small crowds at Formula Two races persuaded wheel manufacturer Hans-Günther Schmid to seek a larger audience for his company's name. He calculated that entering Formula One would cost only a little more. When Roger Penske withdrew his team, Schmid bought its equipment and used it to enter the championship.\n\nSchmid brought personal driving experience to team ownership, having raced in Formula Vee alongside a young Niki Lauda and worked as a Lola agent. His insistence on controlling decisions also created difficulties. Managers and drivers frequently departed after disagreements, disrupting continuity in teams that employed several promising young racers, including Keke Rosberg and Gerhard Berger.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 87,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Full specialist histories support commercial decision, earlier competition and persistent staff turnover. Neutral operational wording replaces colourful character judgments."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S3",
      "S4",
      "S5"
    ],
    "word_count": 104,
    "html": "<p>Small crowds at Formula Two races persuaded wheel manufacturer Hans-Günther Schmid to seek a larger audience for his company's name. He calculated that entering Formula One would cost only a little more. When Roger Penske withdrew his team, Schmid bought its equipment and used it to enter the championship.</p>\r\n<p>Schmid brought personal driving experience to team ownership, having raced in Formula Vee alongside a young Niki Lauda and worked as a Lola agent. His insistence on controlling decisions also created difficulties. Managers and drivers frequently departed after disagreements, disrupting continuity in teams that employed several promising young racers, including Keke Rosberg and Gerhard Berger.</p>\r\n"
  },
